@page "/exsheet/formulas"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Sheet
@using ExSheet.Engine

<DocsPage Product="ExSheet" Title="Formulas"
          Lead="Formulas in Excel's syntax, computed by a declared set of functions that each give Excel's answer. Outside that set, the Sheet says it cannot rather than guess.">

    <DocsSection Title="Typing a Formula">
        <p>
            Text beginning with <code>=</code> is a Formula; anything else is a constant, read under the Sheet's
            culture — <code>1,250.50</code>, <code>4.25%</code> or <code>9/26/2026</code> under en-US. A date or a
            percentage typed as one gives a General cell that format, as in Excel. Formulas are written the same way
            in every culture: <code>,</code> between arguments, <code>.</code> as the decimal separator, English
            function names. The operators are Excel's: <code>+ - * / ^ &amp; %</code> and the comparisons
            <code>= &lt;&gt; &lt; &lt;= &gt; &gt;=</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Enter commits and Escape cancels. A Formula the Sheet cannot read is not committed: the edit stays open,
            and the Sheet says what is wrong. An edit opened with F2 and not typed into closes when its row is
            scrolled far out of view, as ExGrid's does, once the browser has confirmed nothing was typed into it; once
            you type, even as the Sheet scrolls, it stays open until you commit or cancel. Tokens are written in
            Excel's spelling, so <code>=sum(a1,2)</code> is
            kept as <code>=SUM(A1,2)</code>. Recalculation reaches only what a change affects, and Values are
            published only from a completed recalculation.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(Formulas)" Mud="typeof(FormulasMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Sheet/RegionalSales.cs"])">
            Select a total to read its Formula in the Formula Bar, press F2 to edit it, change a quarter's figure and watch the totals, the
            status and the lookup follow, or type another region into B7. Column E, from row 8, shows what the engine refuses.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Arrays that spill">
        <p>
            A Formula whose result is several Values spills, as in Excel 365. The Formula's cell, the Anchor, shows the
            first Value, and the others fill the cells below and to the right, its Spill Range. A Reference to several
            cells spills (<code>=A2:A4</code>), and so does an operator applied to a range, element by element
            (<code>=B2:E2*1.1</code>). So do <code>FILTER</code>, <code>UNIQUE</code>, <code>SORT</code>,
            <code>SORTBY</code>, <code>SEQUENCE</code> and <code>TRANSPOSE</code>, an <code>INDEX</code> with a row or
            a column of 0, and an <code>XLOOKUP</code> whose return array is several columns wide. A function that
            reduces an array takes one and returns one Value: <code>=SUMPRODUCT((A2:A4="North")*F2:F4)</code>.
            <code>INDEX</code> over several rows and columns with <code>column_num</code> left out is
            <code>#REF!</code>, as in Excel, over a Spill Range such as <code>A1#</code> too; write
            <code>=INDEX(G1:H5,2,0)</code> for the row.
        </p>
        <p>
            The cells of a Spill Range hold no Entry. When one of them holds an Entry or another Formula's spill, or
            the range would pass the Sheet's edge, nothing spills and the Anchor shows <code>#SPILL!</code>: nothing is
            overwritten, and clearing the obstacle lets the Formula spill. A Formula that reads a cell of the Spill
            Range follows it as it is blocked and freed, through a Reference that <code>INDIRECT</code> or
            <code>OFFSET</code> computes as well as a typed one. <code>A1#</code> names the Spill Range of
            the Formula in A1. Selecting a spilled cell shows its Anchor's Formula in the Formula Bar, dimmed, because
            it is edited at the Anchor. The <code>@@</code> operator is refused when a Formula is typed: no Formula
            silently takes one Value of a range.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Supported functions">
        <p>
            Each function gives Excel's result. A name outside this set is <code>#NAME?</code>, never an
            approximation of what the function might have meant.
        </p>
        <p>
            The engine supports @DeclaredFunction.All.Count functions. Each one, with its arguments as Excel writes them
            and what it returns, is on the <a href="exsheet/functions">Functions</a> page, by category, with a filter
            and the functions that are not supported yet.
        </p>
        <p>Where the engine cannot give Excel's answer, it gives an Error Value instead of a different answer:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b><code>#CIRC!</code></b> is shown by every cell of a circular reference and every Formula that reads
                one, where Excel shows 0. <code>IFERROR</code> does not catch it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>A function given a range where it takes one Value.</b> Excel 365 applies such a function to each
                Value and spills the results, so <code>=ROUND(B2:E2, -3)</code> gives four. ExSheet does not yet: it is
                <code>#VALUE!</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>An array too large to hold.</b> An array of more than 2<sup>24</sup> Values, sixteen whole columns,
                is not built: whatever would build one is <code>#NUM!</code>. Excel runs out of resources before it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>XLOOKUP</code>'s and <code>XMATCH</code>'s binary search</b> (<code>search_mode</code> 2 and
                -2) answers only over a lookup array sorted as the mode says; otherwise it is <code>#VALUE!</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>A blank among <code>SORT</code>'s or <code>SORTBY</code>'s keys, or in <code>UNIQUE</code>'s
                array,</b> is <code>#VALUE!</code>. Excel sorts it, but where it goes and what its cell shows have not
                been read yet.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>#GETTING_DATA</code></b>, a Linked Table's data still on its way, is not an error to
                <code>IFERROR</code> and <code>ISERROR</code>: a fallback never stands in for data that has not
                arrived. See <MudLink Href="exsheet/linked-tables">Linked Tables</MudLink>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>ERROR.TYPE</code></b> numbers Error Values as Excel does: Microsoft's table, 1 to 7, and
                Excel's newer ones, such as <code>#SPILL!</code> 9 and <code>#CALC!</code> 14. It gives
                <code>#GETTING_DATA</code> and <code>#CIRC!</code> back instead of a number, so that nothing shows a
                figure while its data is on its way.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>SUBTOTAL</code> and <code>AGGREGATE</code></b> answer while a Sheet cannot hide a row, so
                <code>SUBTOTAL</code>'s 101 to 111 give what 1 to 11 give, as Excel's do when nothing is hidden. A cell
                whose Formula calls either anywhere — inside a larger expression, under <code>IF</code>, or in the
                Anchor of the Spill Range it belongs to — is nested, as Excel counts one: <code>SUBTOTAL</code> and
                <code>AGGREGATE</code>'s options 0 to 3 pass over it, and options 4 to 7 count its value. An
                <code>AGGREGATE</code> function number for a function the Sheet does not have, such as 13,
                <code>MODE.SNGL</code>, is <code>#VALUE!</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Arguments that take only a Reference.</b> <code>SUBTOTAL</code>'s references, <code>RANK.EQ</code>'s
                <code>ref</code> and <code>AGGREGATE</code>'s fifth and later arguments refuse, as the Formula is typed,
                what can never be a Reference — a number, a text, an operator's result such as <code>A1:A3*1</code>, a
                call such as <code>SEQUENCE(3)</code> — as Excel refuses <code>=SUBTOTAL(9,5)</code>.
                <code>INDIRECT</code>, <code>OFFSET</code>, <code>INDEX</code>, <code>CHOOSE</code>, <code>IF</code> and
                their kin are taken, since they can give one.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>LARGE</code> and <code>SMALL</code>'s k.</b> <code>LARGE</code> rounds a k that is not whole
                up, as Excel does: <code>=LARGE(A1:B5,1.5)</code> is the 2nd largest. <code>SMALL</code>'s, and a k
                between 0 and 1, are <code>#VALUE!</code> until Excel has been asked. <b><code>MOD</code></b> is
                <code>#NUM!</code> from a quotient of 1,125,900,000,000, the limit reported of Excel 365.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>INDIRECT</code></b> reads A1-style text naming a cell or a range of the same Sheet. Like
                <code>OFFSET</code> it is recalculated after every change, and its text is only text: inserting a row
                rewrites the References beside it, never the text. It also reads what Excel was seen to read: R1C1
                text (<code>a1</code> FALSE) naming a cell or a range, <code>R3C2</code> or <code>R2C2:R5C2</code>; a
                Linked Table's column as text, <code>Positions[PV]</code>, which waits while the table does; and a
                Spill Range, <code>A1#</code>. R1C1 text that is relative, <code>R[1]C[-1]</code>, or names whole rows
                or columns, and a Linked Table's name on its own (<code>"Positions"</code>, which Excel reads as the
                table's data), are <code>#VALUE!</code> until Excel has been asked; text that names no place, another
                Sheet's name or a table that does not exist included, is <code>#REF!</code>, as in Excel.
                What <code>INDIRECT</code> or <code>OFFSET</code> reaches counts as a Reference typed in the Formula
                would: one that reaches its own cell or a circular reference is <code>#CIRC!</code>, and one that
                reaches data still on its way waits, even under <code>COUNT</code> or an <code>AGGREGATE</code>
                option that passes over errors. One that reaches into a Spill Range follows the spill as it is
                blocked and freed.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>LET</code></b> takes the names Excel's keyboard accepts: a letter of any script or
                <code>_</code>, then letters, digits and <code>_</code>, at most 249 characters, and at most 126
                of them. The name of a function is a name too, whether this engine has the function or not
                (<code>sum</code>, <code>irr</code>, <code>n</code>), and so are <code>R1C1</code> and <code>RC</code>;
                a name followed by <code>(</code> is still the function's call. A value may be left empty:
                <code>=LET(x,,x)</code> shows 0, as it does in Excel. A name that reads as a cell, such as
                <code>A1</code> or <code>R2</code>, and <code>TRUE</code> are refused as the Formula is typed, as a
                Formula that cannot be read is, because Excel's keyboard refuses them; so are a name bound twice and
                a missing calculation. A binding is computed only when its name is read,
                but the <code>LET</code> still depends on everything its text names, so an unused binding over
                a cycle shows <code>#CIRC!</code> and one over a Linked Table still on its way waits.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>RAND</code> and <code>RANDBETWEEN</code></b> draw from the Sheet Seed. Each draw is made from
                the seed, the cell, which call in its Formula it is and how many changes the Sheet has had since it
                was opened, so every edit, insertion, deletion, rename of the Sheet, undo, redo, Linked Table
                snapshot and Linked Table declaration draws again,
                while the clock, a format or a width draws nothing. Unlike Excel, nothing draws without a change.
                An undo and a redo draw again rather than bringing back the draws they undo, as Excel's do; a
                rename draws again, as Excel's does, and so do its undo and redo. A snapshot draws again even
                when its rows are the ones the table already held, as Excel's Refresh All does with the file
                unchanged. <code>RANDBETWEEN</code>
                refuses a fractional bound, and one past 2<sup>53</sup>, with <code>#VALUE!</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>PMT</code>, <code>PV</code> and <code>FV</code></b> give, to the last digit, every result Excel
                was seen to give for them. Which result that is depends on how Excel raises 1+rate to the number of
                periods. For <code>PMT</code> it rests on payments at rates of 0.5% to 10% a period. At much smaller
                rates Excel has not been asked, and its payment may differ from ExSheet's from the 13th significant
                digit. At a rate other than 0, a number of periods that is not a whole number, or is negative, is
                <code>#VALUE!</code> until Excel has been asked how it raises to one; so is a <code>type</code> that is
                not a whole number. Any other <code>type</code> but 0, 2 as well as 1, is payment at the start of each
                period, as Excel reads it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>TEXT</code></b> reads its code in the invariant spelling and shows what a cell in that format
                would show, under the Sheet's culture. A code a cell format does not have is <code>#VALUE!</code>,
                though Excel answers it: a fraction such as <code># ?/?</code>, or a condition such as
                <code>[&lt;=9]</code>.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <DocsExample Title="A seeded simulation" Builtin="typeof(SeededDraws)" Mud="typeof(SeededDrawsMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Sheet/DiceRolls.cs"])">
            <code>Seed</code> fixes the Sheet Seed, so the same draws show whenever the document is opened: reload the
            page and they are back. Type into any cell and every draw changes, together with everything that reads it;
            undo, and they change again. Without <code>Seed</code>, ExSheet chooses a seed each time it opens a document;
            a prerendered page chooses none, and its drawing cells show <code>#GETTING_DATA</code> until the page is
            interactive, as <code>TODAY()</code> waits for the browser's time zone, so no draw changes with nothing done.
            Neither the seed nor a draw is saved in the Sheet Document.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Completion, hints and pointing">
        <p>While a Formula is typed, in a cell or in the Formula Bar, the Sheet helps as Excel does:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Completion.</b> Once a letter is typed, matching function names — and the names of Linked Tables —
                are listed; Tab accepts. After <code>Table[</code> the table's columns are listed. An argument that
                takes one of a fixed list, such as <code>XLOOKUP</code>'s <code>match_mode</code>, lists its values
                with Excel's texts, and Tab writes the value.
            </li>
            <li><b>The argument hint</b> shows the function's arguments, with the one the caret is in.</li>
            <li>
                <b>Pointing.</b> Wherever a Reference can go — after <code>=</code>, an operator, <code>(</code> or
                <code>,</code> — the arrow keys and the mouse point at cells and write their Reference. Shift and an
                arrow, or a drag, writes a range. F4 cycles the Reference at the caret through <code>$A$1</code>,
                <code>A$1</code>, <code>$A1</code> and <code>A1</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Reference outlines.</b> Each Reference in the Formula being edited wears a colour, and the cells it
                names are outlined on the Sheet in that colour.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Try it in the sheet above: select an empty cell, type <code>=SU</code>, press Tab, then press ↑ to point
            at a cell and Shift+↑ to make it a range.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The engine on its own">
        <p>
            <code>ExSheet.Engine</code> is the Formula engine with no UI and no dependency. Use it to compute a saved
            Sheet Document on a server, to validate one, or to build a document in code — every Example on this site
            builds its document with it. <code>Enter</code> reads text exactly as a user's typing is read;
            <code>GetDisplay</code> gives what a cell shows, formatted under the Sheet's culture.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(EngineOnly)" CodeOpen="true" />
        <p>
            Every operation a user can perform is a <code>SheetEdit</code> — insert or delete rows and columns, set
            a Cell Format, paste, fill — and <code>Sheet.Do(edit)</code> returns the one <code>SheetStep</code> that
            undoes and redoes it. A paste that writes in parts is still one edit: a copied block over several ranges
            (<code>SheetEdit.Paste(block, targets)</code>), and pasted text some of whose fields carry an exact
            number (<code>SheetEdit.PasteText(fields, entries)</code>). Each is one step, and one change, so
            <code>RAND</code> draws once for it. <code>Sheet.Check(edit)</code> says whether an edit would be refused,
            without doing it. <code>GetDisplay(address, width)</code> gives what the cell shows in a column so many characters wide:
            General rounds to fit, and any other format that does not fit is <code>####</code>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="API">
        <ApiTable Type="typeof(global::ExSheet.Engine.Sheet)" />
